["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Know where the heart and lungs sit, how the bird breathes and what its muscles can do. Then you know where to shoot, how far it will fly, and how to care for the meat.</p></aside><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Side view cutaway of a quail. Crop at the base of the neck. Lungs pressed up under the back. Pale blue air sacs fill much of the body. The heart sits low in the chest behind the crop. The gizzard is lower and further back. The big breast muscle covers the keel underneath. An orange dashed ring marks heart and lungs in the front half of the body. General bird anatomy, 70 percent.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Inside a quail (side view)</text>\n<g transform=\"translate(190 160)\">\n<ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"130\" ry=\"80\" fill=\"#cfd2d6\"/>\n<path d=\"M-125 -10 L-175 -30 L-170 20 Z\" fill=\"#9a9ca2\"/>\n<circle cx=\"110\" cy=\"-80\" r=\"38\" fill=\"#cfd2d6\"/>\n<path d=\"M140 -78 l20 6 l-20 8 Z\" fill=\"#555\"/><circle cx=\"122\" cy=\"-88\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#111\"/>\n<path d=\"M100 -112 q-6 -36 26 -30 q-14 4 -16 30 Z\" fill=\"#111\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"80\" cy=\"-20\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"16\" fill=\"#e8c27a\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"-30\" cy=\"-40\" rx=\"70\" ry=\"22\" fill=\"#bcd3ea\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"-70\" cy=\"10\" rx=\"40\" ry=\"26\" fill=\"#bcd3ea\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"20\" cy=\"-52\" rx=\"34\" ry=\"12\" fill=\"#e79aa0\"/>\n<path d=\"M30 0 q14 -18 28 0 q-14 24 -28 0 Z\" fill=\"#b3222b\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"-20\" cy=\"34\" rx=\"26\" ry=\"20\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/>\n<path d=\"M-20 70 Q60 80 100 30 L100 60 Q60 92 -20 78 Z\" fill=\"#d98c8c\" opacity=\".8\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"40\" cy=\"-14\" rx=\"62\" ry=\"50\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\"/>\n</g>\n<text x=\"270\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">Crop</text>\n<line x1=\"268\" y1=\"124\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Lungs</text>\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Air sacs</text>\n<text x=\"232\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#b3222b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Heart</text>\n<text x=\"168\" y=\"236\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Gizzard</text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"250\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#a0484f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Breast muscle</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"262\" width=\"380\" height=\"32\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"284\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Orange ring: heart and lungs, the front half</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Heart and lungs sit in the front half, under the back and behind the crop <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Most of the body is air sacs, gut and breast muscle <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: how sure is this chapter</summary><ul><li>Quail anatomy is studied far less than chicken anatomy. Quail and chickens are both in the order Galliformes, so this chapter uses poultry anatomy from Poultry Hub Australia (University of New England) and labels it 70% for quail.</li><li>Where a figure is for the chicken (temperature, field of view), the screen says so.</li><li>Shot placement and meat care pull this together on the last screens and in chapter 10.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Chart of a quail flight. White breast muscle, 70 percent: a fast burst on whirring wings, then a glide, then the bird lands and runs. Most flights are short, so mark where the bird lands. White fibres are fast but have little staying power, from poultry anatomy, 70 percent.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">White muscle: burst, glide, land, run</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"40\" width=\"380\" height=\"170\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/>\n<path d=\"M30 190 Q70 80 150 80 L290 120 Q320 150 330 180\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"8 5\"/>\n<g transform=\"translate(30 182) scale(0.9 0.9)\"><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/><path d=\"M-18 -2 L-28 -6 L-26 4 Z\" fill=\"#5f6168\"/><ellipse cx=\"-2\" cy=\"6\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"6\" fill=\"#c9b48a\"/><path d=\"M-10 4 l3 3 M-4 4 l3 3 M2 4 l3 3 M8 4 l3 3\" stroke=\"#5f6168\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M-12 -4 l14 -2 M-12 0 l14 -2\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"-12\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/><path d=\"M14 -10 a7 7 0 0 0 9 6 L22 -2 a9 9 0 0 1 -9 -8 Z\" fill=\"#111\"/><path d=\"M12 -9 q4 9 12 6\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M14 -19 q-2 -10 8 -9 q-4 1 -5 9 Z\" fill=\"#111\"/><circle cx=\"19\" cy=\"-13\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#111\"/><path d=\"M23 -12 l4 1 l-4 2 Z\" fill=\"#333\"/><path d=\"M2 12 l-2 8 M8 12 l2 8\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g>\n<g transform=\"translate(330 172) scale(0.9 0.9)\"><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/><path d=\"M-18 -2 L-28 -6 L-26 4 Z\" fill=\"#5f6168\"/><ellipse cx=\"-2\" cy=\"6\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"6\" fill=\"#c9b48a\"/><path d=\"M-10 4 l3 3 M-4 4 l3 3 M2 4 l3 3 M8 4 l3 3\" stroke=\"#5f6168\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M-12 -4 l14 -2 M-12 0 l14 -2\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"-12\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/><path d=\"M14 -10 a7 7 0 0 0 9 6 L22 -2 a9 9 0 0 1 -9 -8 Z\" fill=\"#111\"/><path d=\"M12 -9 q4 9 12 6\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M14 -19 q-2 -10 8 -9 q-4 1 -5 9 Z\" fill=\"#111\"/><circle cx=\"19\" cy=\"-13\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#111\"/><path d=\"M23 -12 l4 1 l-4 2 Z\" fill=\"#333\"/><path d=\"M2 12 l-2 8 M8 12 l2 8\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g>\n<text x=\"70\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">BURST</text>\n<text x=\"70\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">whirring</text>\n<text x=\"220\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">GLIDE</text>\n<text x=\"340\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">LAND</text>\n<line x1=\"330\" y1=\"192\" x2=\"384\" y2=\"192\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><polygon points=\"384,192 375.0,196.3 375.0,187.7\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<text x=\"350\" y=\"206\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">run</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"236\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">White fibres: fast, short, little staying power (70%)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"258\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Most flights are short (75%)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"282\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">So: mark where it lands, then go (Tip)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Breast muscle is mostly white fibre: fast, short, little staying power <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>So: a whirring burst, a glide, then they land and run <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: bones and muscle in full</summary><ul><li>Bird bones are light. Some are hollow and connect to the air sacs (\"pneumatic bones\") (70%, Poultry Hub).</li><li>The breastbone has a deep keel. The big flight muscles anchor to it <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>In fowl the breast muscles are mostly white fibres: they lack myoglobin, contract fast and tire fast. Fowl fly short distances with very rapid wingbeats (70%, Poultry Hub muscular system).</li><li>Quail run rather than fly, and fly as a last resort (70%, Birder's Handbook via Stanford). When pushed they burst into fast, low flight on whirring wings (75%, Audubon).</li><li>Leg muscles are darker and built for walking and running all day (Tip, 65%: red fibres carry myoglobin for steady work, Poultry Hub).</li><li>Hunter meaning: a quail flight is short. Watch it down. Singles often land a few dozen yards away (75%, Washington Fish and Wildlife).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram anim\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Animation of bird breathing, simplified. 1: a breath in sends fresh air past the lung into the rear air sacs. 2: the breath out pushes that air forward through the lung, where oxygen is taken up. 3: the next breath in moves the air from the lung into the front air sacs. 4: the next breath out sends the used air out of the windpipe. Fresh air crosses the lung on both the in and the out breath. No diaphragm: the ribs and breastbone pump the sacs. Poultry anatomy, 70 percent.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">How a bird breathes: two breaths</text>\n<g transform=\"translate(0 0)\">\n<ellipse cx=\"200\" cy=\"170\" rx=\"170\" ry=\"80\" fill=\"#e9e5dc\"/>\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"150\" width=\"80\" height=\"16\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#9a9ca2\"/>\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"144\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">windpipe</text>\n<rect x=\"160\" y=\"120\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e79aa0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"145\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">LUNG</text>\n<ellipse cx=\"320\" cy=\"190\" rx=\"44\" ry=\"34\" fill=\"#cfe0f0\"/>\n<text x=\"320\" y=\"238\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">rear sacs</text>\n<ellipse cx=\"160\" cy=\"210\" rx=\"40\" ry=\"26\" fill=\"#cfe0f0\"/>\n<text x=\"110\" y=\"250\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">front sacs</text>\n</g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"0.3s\"/><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"0\" begin=\"2.6s\"/><ellipse cx=\"320\" cy=\"190\" rx=\"44\" ry=\"34\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" opacity=\".7\"/><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"158\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><polygon points=\"280,180 270.5,183.1 271.7,174.5\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\"/></g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"2.6s\"/><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"0\" begin=\"5.0s\"/><rect x=\"160\" y=\"120\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e8590c\" opacity=\".6\"/><line x1=\"290\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"145\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><polygon points=\"240,145 250.0,145.9 245.5,153.4\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\"/></g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"5.0s\"/><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"0\" begin=\"7.4s\"/><ellipse cx=\"160\" cy=\"210\" rx=\"40\" ry=\"26\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" opacity=\".7\"/><line x1=\"190\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"190\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><polygon points=\"170,190 171.4,180.1 178.6,184.9\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\"/></g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"7.4s\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"195\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#777\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><polygon points=\"60,160 70.0,159.6 66.5,167.6\" fill=\"#777\"/></g>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"36\" width=\"380\" height=\"34\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"59\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1. Breath in: fresh air goes to the rear sacs</text>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"2.6s\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"36\" width=\"380\" height=\"34\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"59\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2. Breath out: it is pushed through the lung</text></g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"5.0s\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"36\" width=\"380\" height=\"34\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"59\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3. Next breath in: lung air moves to front sacs</text></g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"7.4s\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"36\" width=\"380\" height=\"34\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"59\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4. Next breath out: used air leaves</text></g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"8.6s\"/><rect x=\"60\" y=\"262\" width=\"280\" height=\"32\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"284\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Fresh air crosses the lung both ways (70%)</text></g>\n</svg>\n<figcaption>Two breaths move one gulp of air through the lung. <button class=\"anim-replay\">↻ Replay</button></figcaption></figure><ul><li>No diaphragm. Ribs and breastbone pump nine air sacs <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Fresh air crosses the lung on the breath in and the breath out <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the air sac system in full</summary><ul><li>Fowl have nine air sacs: one clavicular, two cervical, two cranial thoracic, two caudal thoracic and two abdominal (70%, Poultry Hub).</li><li>The air sacs are thin balloons. They hold and move air but do not exchange gas. Gas exchange happens only in the lungs <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>The lungs are small, stiff and pressed up against the ribs on the back <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Breathing: muscles swing the ribs and breastbone forward and back. That changes the body volume and pumps the sacs <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>One gulp of air takes two breaths to pass through: rear sacs, lung, front sacs, out (70%, Poultry Hub; a vet interview in dvm360 describes the same).</li><li>Hunter meaning: the lungs sit high in the back between the ribs, right beside the heart. Pellets that reach the front half of the body hit the engine room (Tip).</li><li>Air sacs fill much of the body cavity. A hit there alone can let a bird fly on (Tip, 60%).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Side view cutaway of a quail. Crop at the base of the neck. Lungs pressed up under the back. Pale blue air sacs fill much of the body. The heart sits low in the chest behind the crop. The gizzard is lower and further back. The big breast muscle covers the keel underneath. An orange dashed ring marks heart and lungs in the front half of the body. General bird anatomy, 70 percent.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Inside a quail (side view)</text>\n<g transform=\"translate(190 160)\">\n<ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"130\" ry=\"80\" fill=\"#cfd2d6\"/>\n<path d=\"M-125 -10 L-175 -30 L-170 20 Z\" fill=\"#9a9ca2\"/>\n<circle cx=\"110\" cy=\"-80\" r=\"38\" fill=\"#cfd2d6\"/>\n<path d=\"M140 -78 l20 6 l-20 8 Z\" fill=\"#555\"/><circle cx=\"122\" cy=\"-88\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#111\"/>\n<path d=\"M100 -112 q-6 -36 26 -30 q-14 4 -16 30 Z\" fill=\"#111\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"80\" cy=\"-20\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"16\" fill=\"#e8c27a\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"-30\" cy=\"-40\" rx=\"70\" ry=\"22\" fill=\"#bcd3ea\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"-70\" cy=\"10\" rx=\"40\" ry=\"26\" fill=\"#bcd3ea\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"20\" cy=\"-52\" rx=\"34\" ry=\"12\" fill=\"#e79aa0\"/>\n<path d=\"M30 0 q14 -18 28 0 q-14 24 -28 0 Z\" fill=\"#b3222b\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"-20\" cy=\"34\" rx=\"26\" ry=\"20\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/>\n<path d=\"M-20 70 Q60 80 100 30 L100 60 Q60 92 -20 78 Z\" fill=\"#d98c8c\" opacity=\".8\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"40\" cy=\"-14\" rx=\"62\" ry=\"50\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\"/>\n</g>\n<text x=\"270\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">Crop</text>\n<line x1=\"268\" y1=\"124\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Lungs</text>\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Air sacs</text>\n<text x=\"232\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#b3222b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Heart</text>\n<text x=\"168\" y=\"236\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Gizzard</text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"250\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#a0484f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Breast muscle</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"262\" width=\"380\" height=\"32\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"284\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Orange ring: heart and lungs, the front half</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Four chambered heart, low in the chest, behind the crop <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Heart and lungs together: about a thumbnail on a quail (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the heart in full</summary><ul><li>The bird heart has two atria and two ventricles, like ours (70%, Poultry Hub circulatory system).</li><li>It sits low in the chest with its point to the rear <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Big vessels leave the heart forward to the neck and wings and back to the body <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>The head and neck carry the brain, spinal cord and big neck vessels. That is why the head and front of the bird is the target in chapter 10 (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Flow chart of a quail digestive tract. Beak picks seeds and leaves. Crop stores a meal. Glandular stomach adds acid. Gizzard grinds food with grit. Gut and two caeca absorb and ferment. Vent passes droppings. Poultry anatomy, 70 percent. Field trick, a Tip: open the crop after the hunt to see what the birds eat right now.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Food path: crop and gizzard</text>\n<rect x=\"14\" y=\"42\" width=\"118\" height=\"74\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/>\n<text x=\"73\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Beak</text>\n<text x=\"73\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">picks seeds, leaves</text>\n<line x1=\"132\" y1=\"79\" x2=\"142\" y2=\"79\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><polygon points=\"142,79 133.0,83.3 133.0,74.7\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<rect x=\"142\" y=\"42\" width=\"118\" height=\"74\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n<text x=\"201\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Crop</text>\n<text x=\"201\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">stores a meal</text>\n<line x1=\"260\" y1=\"79\" x2=\"270\" y2=\"79\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><polygon points=\"270,79 261.0,83.3 261.0,74.7\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<rect x=\"270\" y=\"42\" width=\"118\" height=\"74\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/>\n<text x=\"329\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Stomach</text>\n<text x=\"329\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">acid starts work</text>\n<rect x=\"14\" y=\"138\" width=\"118\" height=\"74\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n<text x=\"73\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Gizzard</text>\n<text x=\"73\" y=\"192\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">grinds with grit</text>\n<line x1=\"132\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"142\" y2=\"175\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><polygon points=\"142,175 133.0,179.3 133.0,170.7\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<rect x=\"142\" y=\"138\" width=\"118\" height=\"74\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/>\n<text x=\"201\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Gut + 2 caeca</text>\n<text x=\"201\" y=\"192\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">absorbs, ferments</text>\n<line x1=\"260\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"270\" y2=\"175\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><polygon points=\"270,175 261.0,179.3 261.0,170.7\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<rect x=\"270\" y=\"138\" width=\"118\" height=\"74\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/>\n<text x=\"329\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Vent</text>\n<text x=\"329\" y=\"192\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">droppings out</text>\n<line x1=\"330\" y1=\"116\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"138\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><polygon points=\"330,138 325.7,129.0 334.3,129.0\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"236\" width=\"380\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"259\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Field trick: open the crop after the hunt.</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"281\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">It shows what they eat right now (Tip)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>The crop stores a meal. The gizzard grinds it with grit <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Open a crop after the hunt: it tells you today's food (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: digestion in full</summary><ul><li>No teeth. Food goes from the beak down the gullet to the crop, a stretchy pouch at the base of the neck that holds a meal (70%, Poultry Hub).</li><li>Then the glandular stomach adds acid and enzymes, and the gizzard, a thick muscle with a horny lining, grinds the food with grit <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Two blind pouches, the caeca, ferment tough plant food <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Young quail pick up the gut microbes they need by eating adult droppings (65%, BC Outdoors).</li><li>California quail eat mostly seeds and green leaves; legume seeds lead the list (80%, California wildlife habitat records). Insects are under 5% of the adult diet (70%, Birder's Handbook) but chicks eat many <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Grit: Washington Fish and Wildlife says quail pick grit at roadside edges before the morning and afternoon feeds <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Gravel lanes next to cover are worth a look.</li><li>In cool weather, quail can get water from green plants, insects and dew <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. In hot weather they need free water every day <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Body heat card. Bird body temperature is about 41 to 41.7 degrees Celsius, chicken figure, 70 percent. Cold: feathers fluffed and quail roost in dense trees or shrubs, not on the ground, 70 percent. Hot: birds pant, rest in shade midday and in hot weather need free water every day, 80 percent from California wildlife habitat records.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Body heat</text>\n<rect x=\"160\" y=\"40\" width=\"80\" height=\"150\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/>\n<rect x=\"190\" y=\"56\" width=\"20\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<rect x=\"194\" y=\"80\" width=\"12\" height=\"76\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"168\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"210\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">41 to 41.7 °C (70%)</text>\n<rect x=\"12\" y=\"40\" width=\"140\" height=\"150\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe8f2\"/>\n<text x=\"82\" y=\"64\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">COLD</text>\n<text x=\"82\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Fluff feathers</text>\n<text x=\"82\" y=\"114\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Roost in thick</text>\n<text x=\"82\" y=\"134\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">trees or shrubs</text>\n<text x=\"82\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">off the ground</text>\n<text x=\"82\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(70%)</text>\n<rect x=\"248\" y=\"40\" width=\"140\" height=\"150\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f6e0d2\"/>\n<text x=\"318\" y=\"64\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">HOT</text>\n<text x=\"318\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Pant, sit in</text>\n<text x=\"318\" y=\"114\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">shade midday</text>\n<text x=\"318\" y=\"134\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Need water</text>\n<text x=\"318\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">every day (80%)</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"232\" width=\"380\" height=\"58\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"256\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hot dry day: hunt near water and shade</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"278\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Cold day: sunny edges near roost trees (Tip)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Body heat about 41 to 41.7 °C in fowl <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Hot day: they pant and need water daily <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: heat in full</summary><ul><li>Fowl body temperature: 41 to 41.7 °C; death near 46 °C (70%, Poultry Hub, chicken figures).</li><li>Feathers and a fat layer under the skin hold heat in the cold <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>In heat, birds pant and flutter the throat to lose heat by evaporation <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>California quail roost in dense trees or shrubs, not on the ground (70%, Birder's Handbook). BC winters are hard on them: one Okanagan naturalist says a bad winter is the main thing that knocks them back (60%, iNFOnews).</li><li>Gray partridge burrow into snow to reach seeds (75%, Audubon). Chukar move down out of heavy snow and onto south slopes (80%, FEIS).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Bar chart from an early California banding study, 60 percent, search preview. Of 101 quail banded, 52 were known alive at 6 months, 15 at 1 year, 4 at 2 years and 1 at 3 years. Lesson: most quail in a fall covey are young of the year.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Few quail live long (early banding study)</text>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<rect x=\"80\" y=\"68.30000000000001\" width=\"44\" height=\"171.7\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n<text x=\"102\" y=\"62.30000000000001\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">101</text>\n<text x=\"102\" y=\"258\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">start</text>\n<rect x=\"142\" y=\"151.60000000000002\" width=\"44\" height=\"88.39999999999999\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n<text x=\"164\" y=\"145.60000000000002\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">52</text>\n<text x=\"164\" y=\"258\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">6 mo</text>\n<rect x=\"204\" y=\"214.5\" width=\"44\" height=\"25.5\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n<text x=\"226\" y=\"208.5\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">15</text>\n<text x=\"226\" y=\"258\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1 yr</text>\n<rect x=\"266\" y=\"233.2\" width=\"44\" height=\"6.8\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n<text x=\"288\" y=\"227.2\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4</text>\n<text x=\"288\" y=\"258\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2 yr</text>\n<rect x=\"328\" y=\"238.3\" width=\"44\" height=\"1.7\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n<text x=\"350\" y=\"232.3\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n<text x=\"350\" y=\"258\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3 yr</text>\n<text x=\"220\" y=\"282\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">101 birds banded: how many were still seen (60%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Few quail live past their first birthday <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Most of a fall covey is young of the year <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: lifespan in full</summary><ul><li>California quail can live about six years, but few live much past one year (65%, BC Outdoors).</li><li>An early California banding study: of 101 birds, 52 were known alive at 6 months, 15 at one year, 4 at two years and 1 at three years (60%, search preview).</li><li>Predators: Cooper's and sharp shinned hawks, great horned owls, snakes, ground squirrels, house cats, coyotes, skunks, bobcats and jays (80%, California wildlife habitat records).</li><li>Numbers rise after wet years with good green growth; rain drives the hatch <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Chukar can live up to 10 years in BC, per one writer (60%, BC Outdoors).</li><li>Hunter meaning: a covey is a renewable crop of young birds. Good spring rain, good fall covey (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Year wheel for California quail in BC. January and February: big winter coveys. March: coveys break up. April: pairs. May: nests. June: peak hatch. July: broods. August: families join together. September: coveys form. October and November: the Region 8 season, 1 October to 30 November, 99 percent. December: winter coveys. Biology 65 to 80 percent.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The quail year</text>\n<path d=\"M200.0 68.0 A96 96 0 0 1 248.0 80.9 L225.0 120.7 A50 50 0 0 0 200.0 114.0 Z\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"218.89379029248403\" y=\"98.48741468089801\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jan</text>\n<text x=\"227.95245687107223\" y=\"62.748159108202486\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">coveys</text>\n<path d=\"M248.0 80.9 A96 96 0 0 1 283.1 116.0 L243.3 139.0 A50 50 0 0 0 225.0 120.7 Z\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"251.618795026618\" y=\"117.38120497338204\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Feb</text>\n<text x=\"276.3675323681471\" y=\"91.21825406947978\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">coveys</text>\n<path d=\"M283.1 116.0 A96 96 0 0 1 296.0 164.0 L250.0 164.0 A50 50 0 0 0 243.3 139.0 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"270.51258531910196\" y=\"150.10620970751597\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mar</text>\n<text x=\"304.31998923921935\" y=\"140.52990503872272\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">break up</text>\n<path d=\"M296.0 164.0 A96 96 0 0 1 283.1 212.0 L243.3 189.0 A50 50 0 0 0 250.0 164.0 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"270.51258531910196\" y=\"187.89379029248403\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Apr</text>\n<text x=\"304.31998923921935\" y=\"197.47009496127728\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">pairs</text>\n<path d=\"M283.1 212.0 A96 96 0 0 1 248.0 247.1 L225.0 207.3 A50 50 0 0 0 243.3 189.0 Z\" fill=\"#b9a88a\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"251.618795026618\" y=\"220.61879502661796\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">May</text>\n<text x=\"276.3675323681471\" y=\"246.78174593052023\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">nests</text>\n<path d=\"M248.0 247.1 A96 96 0 0 1 200.0 260.0 L200.0 214.0 A50 50 0 0 0 225.0 207.3 Z\" fill=\"#b9a88a\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"218.89379029248403\" y=\"239.512585319102\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jun</text>\n<text x=\"227.95245687107223\" y=\"275.25184089179754\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">hatch</text>\n<path d=\"M200.0 260.0 A96 96 0 0 1 152.0 247.1 L175.0 207.3 A50 50 0 0 0 200.0 214.0 Z\" fill=\"#b9a88a\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"181.10620970751597\" y=\"239.512585319102\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jul</text>\n<text x=\"172.04754312892774\" y=\"275.25184089179754\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">broods</text>\n<path d=\"M152.0 247.1 A96 96 0 0 1 116.9 212.0 L156.7 189.0 A50 50 0 0 0 175.0 207.3 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"148.38120497338204\" y=\"220.618795026618\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Aug</text>\n<text x=\"123.63246763185288\" y=\"246.78174593052023\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">families</text>\n<path d=\"M116.9 212.0 A96 96 0 0 1 104.0 164.0 L150.0 164.0 A50 50 0 0 0 156.7 189.0 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"129.48741468089804\" y=\"187.89379029248403\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sep</text>\n<text x=\"95.68001076078063\" y=\"197.4700949612773\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">coveys</text>\n<path d=\"M104.0 164.0 A96 96 0 0 1 116.9 116.0 L156.7 139.0 A50 50 0 0 0 150.0 164.0 Z\" fill=\"#e8590c\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"129.487414680898\" y=\"150.10620970751597\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Oct</text>\n<text x=\"95.68001076078062\" y=\"140.52990503872272\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"end\">SEASON</text>\n<path d=\"M116.9 116.0 A96 96 0 0 1 152.0 80.9 L175.0 120.7 A50 50 0 0 0 156.7 139.0 Z\" fill=\"#e8590c\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"148.381204973382\" y=\"117.38120497338204\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Nov</text>\n<text x=\"123.63246763185285\" y=\"91.21825406947978\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"end\">SEASON</text>\n<path d=\"M152.0 80.9 A96 96 0 0 1 200.0 68.0 L200.0 114.0 A50 50 0 0 0 175.0 120.7 Z\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"181.106209707516\" y=\"98.48741468089801\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Dec</text>\n<text x=\"172.04754312892777\" y=\"62.748159108202486\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">coveys</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Oct 1 to</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Nov 30</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(99%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Fat cycle for California quail in BC: no good data. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Tip: birds in late November often feel plumper than in early October.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: what we know and do not know</summary><ul><li>No source found that measures California quail fat by month in BC. Treat any claim as a Tip (<span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>).</li><li>What is known: breeding and moult cost energy in spring and summer; seeds are the main fall and winter food (80%, California wildlife habitat records).</li><li>Tip: weigh a few cleaned birds early and late in the season and write it in the Journal. Your own numbers beat a guess.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The shot window. You stand in brush. A wedge of open sky above and ahead of you is the safe window. A high bird with sky behind it: OK. A low bird over the brush: no shot. Range about 15 to 30 metres, a Tip. Loads from Washington Fish and Wildlife, 75 percent: lead 7.5 or 8, steel 6 or 7, skeet or improved cylinder choke. Straightaway birds: most hunters shoot high, so hold a hair under.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The shot window</text>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"40\" width=\"400\" height=\"170\" fill=\"#dbe8f4\"/>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"170\" width=\"400\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n<g transform=\"translate(200 228)\"><circle r=\"10\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"0\" y=\"-16\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">You</text></g>\n<path d=\"M200 220 L80 60 L320 60 Z\" fill=\"#e8590c\" opacity=\".18\"/>\n<path d=\"M200 220 L80 60 M200 220 L320 60\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 5\"/>\n<g transform=\"translate(200 90)\"><ellipse rx=\"10\" ry=\"6\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/></g>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">high bird, sky behind: OK</text>\n<g transform=\"translate(330 180)\"><ellipse rx=\"10\" ry=\"6\" fill=\"#7d7f86\"/></g>\n<text x=\"330\" y=\"164\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#a33b2a\" text-anchor=\"middle\">low bird: NO</text>\n<text x=\"70\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">15 to 30 m</text>\n<text x=\"70\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(Tip)</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"246\" width=\"380\" height=\"46\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"266\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Lead 7.5 or 8 (steel 6 or 7), open choke</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"286\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Straightaway: hold a hair under (75%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Small bird, small shot: lead 7.5 or 8, steel 6 or 7 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Aim for the head and front half: brain, neck, heart, lungs (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: shot and meat in full</summary><ul><li>One small pellet is often enough to bring down a quail (75%, Washington Fish and Wildlife).</li><li>A pattern that puts several pellets in the head and front body kills cleanly. A shot to the rear catches gut and air sacs: the bird may fly on (Tip).</li><li>Breast meat is white and lean, so it dries out fast in the pan (Tip). Legs are darker and tougher (Tip, 65%).</li><li>Gut contents and the crop sour fast in warm weather. Cool birds fast (Tip). Chapter 10 has the steps.</li><li>Both breasts are <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Edible portions\">Edible portions</button> and must go home (99%, Rule Book).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Side view cutaway of a quail. Crop at the base of the neck. Lungs pressed up under the back. Pale blue air sacs fill much of the body. The heart sits low in the chest behind the crop. The gizzard is lower and further back. The big breast muscle covers the keel underneath. An orange dashed ring marks heart and lungs in the front half of the body. General bird anatomy, 70 percent.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Inside a quail (side view)</text>\n<g transform=\"translate(190 160)\">\n<ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"130\" ry=\"80\" fill=\"#cfd2d6\"/>\n<path d=\"M-125 -10 L-175 -30 L-170 20 Z\" fill=\"#9a9ca2\"/>\n<circle cx=\"110\" cy=\"-80\" r=\"38\" fill=\"#cfd2d6\"/>\n<path d=\"M140 -78 l20 6 l-20 8 Z\" fill=\"#555\"/><circle cx=\"122\" cy=\"-88\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#111\"/>\n<path d=\"M100 -112 q-6 -36 26 -30 q-14 4 -16 30 Z\" fill=\"#111\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"80\" cy=\"-20\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"16\" fill=\"#e8c27a\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"-30\" cy=\"-40\" rx=\"70\" ry=\"22\" fill=\"#bcd3ea\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"-70\" cy=\"10\" rx=\"40\" ry=\"26\" fill=\"#bcd3ea\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"20\" cy=\"-52\" rx=\"34\" ry=\"12\" fill=\"#e79aa0\"/>\n<path d=\"M30 0 q14 -18 28 0 q-14 24 -28 0 Z\" fill=\"#b3222b\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"-20\" cy=\"34\" rx=\"26\" ry=\"20\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/>\n<path d=\"M-20 70 Q60 80 100 30 L100 60 Q60 92 -20 78 Z\" fill=\"#d98c8c\" opacity=\".8\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"40\" cy=\"-14\" rx=\"62\" ry=\"50\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\"/>\n</g>\n<text x=\"270\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">Crop</text>\n<line x1=\"268\" y1=\"124\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Lungs</text>\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Air sacs</text>\n<text x=\"232\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#b3222b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Heart</text>\n<text x=\"168\" y=\"236\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Gizzard</text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"250\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#a0484f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Breast muscle</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"262\" width=\"380\" height=\"32\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"284\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Orange ring: heart and lungs, the front half</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li><strong>Rhyme:</strong> \"Front half, fast fall; rear end, no kill at all.\" (Tip)</li><li><strong>Number trick:</strong> 9 air sacs, 4 heart chambers, 2 breaths per gulp <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: more hooks</summary><ul><li><strong>Crop check:</strong> \"What's in the crop is what's on the slope.\" Open one crop after every hunt (Tip).</li><li><strong>Muscle hook:</strong> white meat flies, dark meat walks (Tip, 65%).</li><li><strong>Old hand's rule (opinion):</strong> \"Watch it down. A quail never flies as far as you think.\" (Tip, backed by short flights, 75%)</li></ul></details>","<ul><li>Poultry Hub Australia (University of New England), Audubon, California wildlife habitat records, Washington Fish and Wildlife, BC Outdoors. Checked 2026-10-11.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: every source and date checked</summary><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.poultryhub.org/anatomy-and-physiology/body-systems/respiratory-system-thermoregulation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Poultry Hub Australia: respiratory system and thermoregulation</a>, <a href=\"https://www.poultryhub.org/anatomy-and-physiology/body-systems/digestive-system\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">digestive system</a>, <a href=\"https://www.poultryhub.org/anatomy-and-physiology/body-systems/circulatory-system\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">circulatory system</a>, <a href=\"https://www.poultryhub.org/anatomy-and-physiology/body-systems/skeletal-system\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">skeletal system</a>, <a href=\"https://www.poultryhub.org/anatomy-and-physiology/body-systems/muscular-system\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">muscular system</a>, read directly 2026-10-11. Chicken anatomy applied to quail <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.dvm360.com/view/the-unique-avian-respiratory-system\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">dvm360: the unique avian respiratory system</a>, read directly 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://ncrcd.specialdistrict.org/files/b900df31f/California-Quail-info.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">California Wildlife Habitat Relationships: California quail (California Department of Fish and Game)</a>, read directly 2026-10-11: diet, water, predators, rainfall <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/group/stanfordbirds/text/species/California_Quail.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Stanford Birds: California quail, from The Birder's Handbook (Ehrlich, Dobkin and Wheye 1988)</a>, read directly 2026-10-11: roost, running, diet <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://wdfw.wa.gov/node/68963\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Washington Fish and Wildlife: quail hunting</a>, read directly 2026-10-11: grit, singles, shot size <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.audubon.org/field-guide/bird/california-quail\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Audubon field guide: California quail</a> and <a href=\"https://www.audubon.org/field-guide/bird/gray-partridge\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">gray partridge</a>, read directly 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.fs.usda.gov/database/feis/animals/bird/alch/all.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">US Forest Service FEIS: chukar (Sullivan 1994)</a>, read directly 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.bcoutdoorsmagazine.com/?p=9278\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Outdoors: California quail (Harris 2021)</a>, read directly 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>. <a href=\"https://infonews.ca/?p=13774\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">iNFOnews 2018</a> <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Banding study: an early California quail banding study at Hart Park, California, seen only as a search preview 2026-10-11. Exact paper and year: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Rules: <a href=\"#/s/rb-unlawful-acts\">Rule Book: unlawful acts, item 44</a>, 2 October 2026 edition <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li></ul></details>"]